---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Security — MeshWhisper"
  description="PQXDH, Double Ratchet, safety numbers, and the post-quantum roadmap."
>
  <Nav current="Security" />

  <main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-20">

    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Security</h1>
    <p class="text-slate-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-16">
      Every app that integrates MeshWhisper gets post-quantum E2EE without having to understand or
      implement cryptography. These are the primitives underneath.
    </p>

    <!-- Level table -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-slate-800">
              <th class="text-left text-slate-500 font-medium py-3 pr-6">Level</th>
              <th class="text-left text-slate-500 font-medium py-3 pr-6">What's protected</th>
              <th class="text-left text-slate-500 font-medium py-3">Status</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800/60">
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-6 text-white font-medium">1</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-6 text-slate-400">Classical E2EE — AES-256-GCM per message</td>
              <td class="py-3 text-brand-400 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide">Done</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-6 text-white font-medium">2</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-6 text-slate-400">Post-quantum at session establishment (PQXDH)</td>
              <td class="py-3 text-brand-400 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide">Done</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-3 pr-6 text-white font-medium">3</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-6 text-slate-400">Post-quantum healing within live sessions (PQ3 ratchet)</td>
              <td class="py-3 text-yellow-500 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide">Roadmap</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-500 text-xs mt-4">
        Level classification matches Apple's iMessage PQ3 security levels. Signal is Level 2. Apple iMessage is Level 3.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- PQXDH -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">PQXDH — post-quantum initial key exchange</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Session establishment uses a hybrid of X3DH and ML-KEM-768. When Bob's pre-key bundle includes an
        ML-KEM-768 public key, Alice encapsulates to it during the handshake. The final shared secret mixes
        all X25519 DH outputs with the ML-KEM shared secret under a BLAKE3 domain separation context.
        This matches Signal's PQXDH specification.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The protection PQXDH provides: an adversary who records encrypted traffic today cannot decrypt it
        with a future quantum computer, because the session key was derived from an ML-KEM encapsulation
        that a quantum computer cannot reverse. This is the "harvest now, decrypt later" threat model.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        The limitation of PQXDH alone: if a session's ratchet state is compromised after establishment,
        future traffic can be decrypted with classical compute. This is what Level 3 ratchet healing addresses.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Double Ratchet -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Double Ratchet</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Session encryption uses the Signal Double Ratchet algorithm. Every message uses a fresh key derived
        from the previous one via a symmetric-key ratchet. When a new message arrives from the other side,
        a DH ratchet step advances the root chain using a fresh X25519 key exchange, providing
        break-in recovery: forward secrecy and break-in recovery simultaneously.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        The implementation handles out-of-order delivery via a skipped-message-key store with a
        MAX_SKIP cap of 2,000 — sized to cover the relay's 72-hour store-and-forward TTL without
        bounding memory consumption unreasonably.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- PQ3 roadmap -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">PQ3 ratchet healing — Level 3</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Level 3 adds periodic ML-KEM injection into the running Double Ratchet. Every 50 messages — or
        every 24 hours, whichever comes first — one side advertises a new ML-KEM-768 public key in the
        message header. The other side encapsulates to it and includes the ciphertext in the next reply.
        Both sides mix the resulting shared secret into the root key.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The effect: even if an adversary compromises ratchet state mid-conversation, post-quantum forward
        secrecy is restored at the next injection point. The exposure window is bounded by the injection
        cadence rather than the session lifetime.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-yellow-800/40 rounded-xl p-5 text-sm text-slate-400">
        <span class="text-yellow-500 font-medium">Roadmap.</span> The spec is written — see{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/antonmannering/meshwhisper/blob/main/docs/pq3-ratchet-spec.md" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300 transition-colors">docs/pq3-ratchet-spec.md</a>.
        Implementation affects <code class="text-slate-300">src/ratchet/index.ts</code>,{' '}
        <code class="text-slate-300">src/sdk/utils.ts</code>, and{' '}
        <code class="text-slate-300">src/sdk/session-manager.ts</code>.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Safety numbers -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Safety numbers</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Both parties can verify a 60-digit safety number out-of-band to confirm no man-in-the-middle.
        The number is computed as a sorted BLAKE3 hash of both parties' Ed25519 identity keys, formatted
        as 12 groups of 5 decimal digits. It is identical regardless of which party calls it first.
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 font-mono text-sm text-slate-400 my-4">
        <span class="text-slate-500">// Example</span><br />
        <span class="text-brand-300">mw.getSafetyNumber(peerId)</span><br />
        <span class="text-slate-500">// "12345 67890 12345 67890 12345 67890 12345 67890 12345 67890 12345 67890"</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Threat model -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">What the node knows</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        A node operator can observe source IPs of connected devices, push tokens for offline delivery,
        destination hashes (which rotate hourly), and traffic timing. It cannot read message content,
        identify senders or recipients by name, or correlate traffic across namespaces.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
        Private keys are generated on-device and never transmitted. The node never holds a session key,
        a message key, or any key material derived from the Double Ratchet or PQXDH handshake.
        Media is encrypted locally before upload — the node stores ciphertext and never receives the
        decryption key.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Primitives -->
    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Primitives</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-sm">
        {[
          ['@noble/curves', 'X25519, Ed25519 — audited pure-JS elliptic curve operations'],
          ['@noble/ciphers', 'AES-256-GCM — authenticated encryption'],
          ['@noble/hashes', 'BLAKE3 — KDF, destination hash derivation, safety numbers'],
          ['@noble/post-quantum', 'ML-KEM-768 — NIST FIPS 203, PQXDH encapsulation'],
        ].map(([pkg, desc]) => (
          <div class="flex gap-4 items-baseline">
            <code class="text-brand-300 w-44 shrink-0">{pkg}</code>
            <span class="text-slate-400">{desc}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-500 text-xs mt-6">
        All primitives are pure JavaScript with no WASM or native bindings. Identical behaviour in browser, Node.js, and React Native.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="border-t border-slate-800 pt-8 flex gap-6 text-sm">
      <a href="/how-it-works" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300 transition-colors">← How it works</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/antonmannering/meshwhisper/blob/main/docs/pq3-ratchet-spec.md" class="text-slate-500 hover:text-slate-300 transition-colors">PQ3 ratchet spec →</a>
    </div>

  </main>

  <footer class="border-t border-slate-800 mt-12">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-8 text-sm text-slate-600 flex justify-between">
      <span>MeshWhisper — MIT licensed</span>
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-slate-400 transition-colors">← Home</a>
    </div>
  </footer>

</Layout>
